  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Calculate $AB+AC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB+AC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |",
  "worked_solution": "We can apply the distributive law\n\n$$\nAB+AC=A(B+C).\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $B+C$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B+C & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & \\sqrt{3}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB+AC & =A(B+C) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{3} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
